  "query_preview": "forecast. Currently the upper level ridge that has\ncontrolled our weather is centered over the New Mexico/Arizona \nborder. During the day on Thursday, the upper level ridge will \ndrift north to near the Four Corners region and that will allow \nour precipitable water values (PW's) to climb a bit as moisture is\nable to work into the region from the east. This increased \nmoisture will help fuel an uptick in thunderstorm activity, so \nmost of the area will have a chance for thunderstorms on Thursday,\nbut the best chances for rain will be west of the Rio Grande. \nHigh temperatures Thursday will continue to run 5 to 8 degrees \nabove average. \n\nBy Friday and into the weekend, drier air will return to the\nregion and we will see lower rain chances across the area. The\nGila Wilderness will continue to see at least a slight chance for\nrain, but the rest of will likely stay dry through Sunday. High \ntemperatures for Friday, Saturday and Sunday will run 3 to 5 \ndegrees above average. Most lowland locations will be at or a \nlittle below the triple digit mark for high temperatures. \n\nFor Monday into the Tuesday, the extended models are beginning to\nindicate a pattern change. The upper level ridge over the\nSouthwest will begin to weaken, while the Bermuda high over the\nSoutheast U.S. will begin to strengthen. This will be a slow\nprocess, but by Wednesday, we begin to see signs of a true monsoon\npattern setting up over the desert Southwest. We will see what \nlater runs of the models bring.",
